// ============================================================================
// 1. INTRODUCTION
// ============================================================================

= Introduction

== Lab Overview

Lab 14 used a motor driver module to configure the PWM level, direction, and
stop state of a DC motor. However, the PWM value represents only the control
command generated by the microcontroller and does not directly indicate the
actual mechanical speed of the motor.

In systems that require motion observation, an encoder can be used to provide
feedback from a rotating shaft. By counting encoder state transitions during a
defined time interval, the program can calculate rotational speed in
revolutions per minute (RPM). A two-channel encoder can also determine the
rotation direction from the phase relationship between the two signals
#cite-ref(refs, "ni-encoder") #cite-ref(refs, "rockwell-encoder").

This lab adds a two-channel A/B encoder to the motor system introduced in
Lab 14. The motor is driven continuously at a fixed PWM level, while
EduFramework measures the encoder signals and sends the actual RPM value to the
Serial Monitor through `Serial1`.

== Objectives

#objectives(
  items: (
    [
      Describe the role of an encoder in measuring rotational motion of a
      motor.
    ],

    [
      Explain the basic operating principle of a two-channel incremental A/B
      encoder and quadrature x4 decoding.
    ],

    [
      Determine the number of counts per revolution from the encoder pulse
      specification.
    ],

    [
      Use the Encoder Device API to update and read the motor RPM.
    ],

    [
      Display the motor speed in real time on the Serial Monitor.
    ],
