)


// ============================================================================
// 2. BACKGROUND
// ============================================================================

= Background

== Encoder in Motion Measurement Systems

An encoder is a device that generates electrical signals corresponding to
mechanical motion. In rotating systems, an encoder is commonly attached to a
motor shaft to provide information about position, motion count, direction, or
speed #cite-ref(refs, "umich-eecs461") #cite-ref(refs, "rockwell-encoder").

Encoders can use different sensing principles. Common technologies include
optical, magnetic, and capacitive encoders
#cite-ref(refs, "encoder-technologies"). This lab uses an optical encoder
integrated directly with the DC motor.

In an optical encoder, a disk containing periodic slots or patterns rotates with
the motor shaft. A light source and optical receiver convert the variation in
light into a sequence of electrical pulses. A two-channel arrangement allows
the system to determine not only motion count but also the direction of
rotation #cite-ref(refs, "tue-optical-encoder").

== Two-Channel Incremental A/B Encoder

The encoder used in this lab provides two channels, A and B. These signals are
periodic and phase-shifted relative to each other. The order in which A and B
change depends on the direction of rotation, allowing the decoder to determine
motion in both directions #cite-ref(refs, "ni-encoder").

The channel states can be visualized as logic pairs:

`00` → `01` → `11` → `10` → `00`

When the rotation direction reverses, the transition order also reverses.
EduFramework tracks valid state transitions and increments or decrements the
encoder count accordingly #cite-ref(refs, "eduframework-encoder").

== Quadrature x4 Decoding and CPR

A complete cycle of a two-channel encoder contains four edge transitions that
can be counted. When both rising and falling edges of channels A and B are used,
the method is referred to as quadrature x4 decoding
#cite-ref(refs, "ni-encoder").

The encoder integrated with the motor used in this lab produces `30` pulses per
revolution. EduFramework uses x4 decoding, so the number of counts per
mechanical revolution is:

$ 30 times 4 = 120 $

Therefore, the `countsPerRevolution` value passed to `Encoder_Init()` is
`120U`.

#note[
  The CPR value passed to the Encoder Device API must represent the number of
  decoded counts per mechanical revolution, not only the nominal number of
  encoder pulses.
]

== RPM Calculation

Rotational speed can be calculated from the number of counts that change during
a defined time interval. The general principle is to measure the count change
within a sampling window, then convert the result using CPR and the elapsed time
to obtain revolutions per minute #cite-ref(refs, "ti-eqep-speed").

In EduFramework, the calculation can be represented by the following
relationship:

`RPM = |ΔCount| × 60000 / (CPR × Δt_ms)`

where:

- `ΔCount` is the count change during the measurement window;
- `CPR` is the number of counts per revolution;
- `Δt_ms` is the measurement-window duration in milliseconds;
- `60000` is the number of milliseconds in one minute.

RPM is returned as a positive magnitude. Rotation direction is managed
separately by the Encoder Device API.


