// ============================================================================
// 4. EDUFRAMEWORK API
// ============================================================================

= EduFramework API

Lab 15 reuses the DC Motor Device API from Lab 14 to create motion and focuses
on the new Encoder Device API. The main APIs used in the exercise are
`Encoder_Init()`, `Encoder_Reset()`, `Encoder_Update()`, and `Encoder_GetRpm()`
#cite-ref(refs, "eduframework-encoder").

== `Encoder_Init()`

`Encoder_Init()` initializes the two encoder channels and configures the number
of counts per revolution.

#api-detail(
  name: "Encoder_Init",

  syntax: [Encoder_Init(channelAPin, channelBPin, countsPerRevolution);],

  description: [
    Initialize a two-channel encoder and configure the number of decoded counts
    per mechanical revolution.
  ],

  parameters: (
    (
      [channelAPin],
      [Logical Pin],
      [Pin connected to encoder Channel A.],
    ),

    (
      [channelBPin],
      [Logical Pin],
      [Pin connected to encoder Channel B.],
    ),

    (
      [countsPerRevolution],
      [Count / revolution],
      [Number of decoded counts per mechanical revolution.],
    ),
  ),

  returns: [
    Returns `1U` when initialization is successful and `0U` when the
    configuration is invalid.
  ],
)

Example:

```c
Encoder_Init(GPIO8, GPIO9, 120U);
```

== `Encoder_Reset()`

`Encoder_Reset()` resets the accumulated count, RPM value, and rotation
direction state to their initial values.

#api-detail(
  name: "Encoder_Reset",

  syntax: [Encoder_Reset();],

  description: [
    Reset the encoder measurement state.
  ],

  parameters: (),

  returns: [No return value.],
)

== `Encoder_Update()`

`Encoder_Update()` updates the speed measurement using the encoder count and
elapsed time. The function is non-blocking and should be called repeatedly from
the main loop.

#api-detail(
  name: "Encoder_Update",

  syntax: [Encoder_Update();],

  description: [
    Update the latest encoder RPM and rotation direction.
  ],

  parameters: (),

  returns: [No return value.],
)

In the current EduFramework version, RPM is updated using an internal
measurement window. The application can continue performing other tasks between
updates.

== `Encoder_GetRpm()`

`Encoder_GetRpm()` returns the latest rotational speed calculated by
`Encoder_Update()`.

#api-detail(
  name: "Encoder_GetRpm",

  syntax: [Encoder_GetRpm();],

  description: [
    Read the latest encoder rotational speed.
  ],

  parameters: (),

  returns: [Rotational speed in revolutions per minute (RPM).],
)

EduFramework also provides `Encoder_GetCount()` and `Encoder_GetDirection()` to
read the accumulated count and rotation direction. These APIs are not required
in the main exercise but can be used when additional encoder state information
is needed.


// ============================================================================
// 5. LAB EXERCISE
// ============================================================================

= Lab Exercise

== Requirements

Build a program that runs the motor continuously at approximately `50%` control
level, measures the speed using the encoder, and displays the RPM on the Serial
Monitor.

The program must meet the following requirements:

- Initialize Serial1 at `9600 bps`.
- Initialize the motor-control system using `GPIO2` through `GPIO5`.
- Initialize the encoder with Channel A on `GPIO8`, Channel B on `GPIO9`, and
  `120` counts per revolution.
- Set the motor speed-control value to `128`.
- Run the motor continuously in the Forward direction.
- Call `Encoder_Update()` continuously in the main loop.
- Display the RPM on the Serial Monitor every `200 ms`.

== Program

In `src/main.c`, implement the program as follows:

#block(breakable: false)[
  #code-listing(
    caption: [Motor speed measurement program using an encoder],
  )[
    ```c
    #include "Arduino.h"
    #include "hardware_serial.h"
    #include "dc_motor.h"
    #include "encoder.h"

    int main(void)
    {
        uint32_t previousTime = 0U;

        setup();

        Serial1_begin(9600U);

        DCMotor_Init(GPIO2, GPIO3, GPIO4, GPIO5);

        if (0U == Encoder_Init(GPIO8, GPIO9, 120U))
        {
            Serial1_println("Encoder initialization failed.");

            while (1)
            {
            }
        }

        Encoder_Reset();

        DCMotor_SetSpeed(128U);
        DCMotor_Forward();

        Serial1_println("Motor speed measurement started.");

        while (1)
        {
            Encoder_Update();

            if ((millis() - previousTime) >= 200U)
            {
                previousTime = millis();

                Serial1_print("RPM: ");
                Serial1_printFloat(Encoder_GetRpm());
                Serial1_println("");
            }
        }

        return 0;
    }
    ```
  ]
]

== Program Explanation

After `setup()`, `Serial1_begin(9600U)` initializes the Serial Monitor. The motor
control section continues to use the configuration introduced in Lab 14.

`Encoder_Init(GPIO8, GPIO9, 120U)` configures the two encoder channels and
declares `120` counts per revolution. If initialization fails, the program
prints an error message and does not continue to the measurement stage.

After `Encoder_Reset()` resets the measurement state,
`DCMotor_SetSpeed(128U)` sets the control level to approximately `50%`, and
`DCMotor_Forward()` keeps the motor rotating continuously.

Inside the main loop, `Encoder_Update()` is called continuously to update the
speed measurement. Every `200 ms`, the program reads the latest value using
`Encoder_GetRpm()` and sends the result to the Serial Monitor.

Separating the Serial update timing from the encoder measurement allows the
main loop to continue calling `Encoder_Update()` frequently instead of blocking
the program with `delay()`.

== Verification

Build and upload the program to the MaaZEDU Development Board. Apply external
power to the motor, open the Serial Monitor at `9600 bps`, and observe the
output.

Example:

```text
Motor speed measurement started.
RPM: 2115.000
RPM: 2130.000
RPM: 2125.000
RPM: 2130.000
```

The actual RPM depends on the motor, supply voltage, mechanical load, and
operating conditions, so it is not required to match the example exactly.

#expected-result[
  The motor rotates continuously in one direction at control value `128`. The
  Serial Monitor displays the RPM measured by the encoder and updates the value
  periodically at approximately `200 ms`. When the motor speed becomes stable,
  consecutive RPM values fluctuate around a relatively stable level.
]


// ============================================================================
// 6. EXTENSION
// ============================================================================

= Extension

The main exercise uses a fixed PWM level to focus on encoder-based speed
measurement. By combining UART input data with RPM feedback, the program can be
extended into an interactive motor-speed control application.

== Adjusting the PWM Level through UART

Extend the program so that the user can enter a value from `0` to `255` through
the Serial Monitor. The received value is used as the new control level for
`DCMotor_SetSpeed()`.

After each change in the control value, the encoder continues to measure the
actual speed and the Serial Monitor displays the corresponding RPM.

The intended processing flow is:

`UART Input` → `PWM Command` → `DC Motor` → `Encoder` → `Measured RPM`

*Hints:*

- Check incoming data from `Serial1`.
- Convert the received data into a numeric value.
- Verify that the value is within the `0..255` range.
- Update the control level using `DCMotor_SetSpeed()`.
- Continue calling `Encoder_Update()` in the main loop.
- Display both the current PWM value and the actual RPM for comparison.

An automatic algorithm that adjusts PWM according to RPM is not required. The
user directly changes the control command and observes the measured motor-speed
response.

#expected-result[
  The user can enter different control levels through the Serial Monitor. The
  motor speed changes accordingly, and the encoder provides the actual RPM so
  that the effect of each PWM level can be observed.
]


